        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1473,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 729,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 702,
            "height": 661,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "- subtracts the operand from every value that arrives at its left inlet.\n\n7 arriving at \"- 3\" sends out 4. A sine wave swinging from -1 to 1, sent through\n\"- 0.5\", swings from -1.5 to 0.5: the same shape, lowered.\n\nUse it to remove an offset: subtract a sensor's resting value so it reads 0 at rest,\nor subtract a threshold so that only the excess above it stays positive.\n\nArrays and tensors are handled element by element, keeping their shape. A single-number\noperand applies to every element; an array operand of the same shape pairs element with\nelement. In the NumPy example at the bottom left,\none random array is subtracted from the other cell by cell, so the result runs\nfrom -1 to 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\n- <operand: int or float>\n\nEXAMPLES:\n- 60       a note number measured from middle C\n- 0.5      centre a 0 to 1 control around zero\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe value to subtract from. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\noperand:\nThe amount taken away. \nSetting it does not send anything - it waits for the next value at \"in\".\n\"- 1\" gives a stepped integer slider; \"- 1.0\" a smooth floating point one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe input minus the operand.\n\nORDER MATTERS:\n- always takes the operand away from the input. To take the input away from\nthe operand instead - to turn a 0 to 1 control into 1 to 0, say - use !-.\n\nSEE ALSO:\n+     add the operand\n!-    the operand minus the input",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 694,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 645,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "- subtracts the operand from every value that arrives at its left inlet.\n\n7 arriving at \"- 3\" sends out 4. A sine wave swinging from -1 to 1, sent through\n\"- 0.5\", swings from -1.5 to 0.5: the same shape, lowered.\n\nUse it to remove an offset: subtract a sensor's resting value so it reads 0 at rest,\nor subtract a threshold so that only the excess above it stays positive.\n\nArrays and tensors are handled element by element, keeping their shape. A single-number\noperand applies to every element; an array operand of the same shape pairs element with\nelement. In the NumPy example at the bottom left,\none random array is subtracted from the other cell by cell, so the result runs\nfrom -1 to 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\n- <operand: int or float>\n\nEXAMPLES:\n- 60       a note number measured from middle C\n- 0.5      centre a 0 to 1 control around zero\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe value to subtract from. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\noperand:\nThe amount taken away. \nSetting it does not send anything - it waits for the next value at \"in\".\n\"- 1\" gives a stepped integer slider; \"- 1.0\" a smooth floating point one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe input minus the operand.\n\nORDER MATTERS:\n- always takes the operand away from the input. To take the input away from\nthe operand instead - to turn a 0 to 1 control into 1 to 0, say - use !-.\n\nSEE ALSO:\n+     add the operand\n!-    the operand minus the input"
        },
